From: eisrael@suneast.East.Sun.COM (Elias Israel - SunSelect Engineering)
Subject: ECON: Tragedy of the commons

Dale Worley writes:
> Or here's a tricky one:  Who "owns" an endangered species?  Should we
> have a system where an entity can buy up an endangered species, thus
> acquiring a property interest in protecting it?

Sure, why not? Already there are a number of land conservation groups that
operate primarily or wholly by soliciting funds from members and then buying
up land that they want to protect. (The Nature Conservancy is one, for
example.)

I can think of no reason, absent government prohibitions, why the same
thing couldn't be done for the Northern Spotted DungMoose, or whatever.
In fact, I believe this has been tried to a limited degree in Africa to
protect elephants from ivory poachers to good effect.

