This sounds like the vision of Hell in the popular Christian apologist C.S.
Lewis's _Perelandra_, the second volume of his _Out_of_the_Silent_Planet_
science fiction trilogy.  A physicist--who has allowed himself to come under
the Devil's direct control in the second book--in the first volume gives as
his goal something like "boundless expansion" when questioned on his motives
by the hyperdimensional being, i.e., angel, who rules Mars.

That nearly half a century ago Professor Lewis parodied central extropian
ideas makes me want to investigate whether he was prescient or what extropian
predecessors unknown to me his attack suggests existed.  He mentions Olaf
Stapledon as a man whose invention he admires though whose philosopy he abhors
in an introduction, but I don't think the two Stapledon books I've read,
_Last_and_First_Men_ and _Starmaker_, are proto-extropian.  The references to
_Odd_John_ I've read suggest that it isn't either.  I vaguely remember hearing
that a book containing wild technological speculation by J.B.S. Haldane, _The_
_World,_the_Flesh,_and_the_Devil_, interested Lewis; and I think it came out
in the '20's or '30's and therefore predates Lewis's trilogy; however, I
haven't found a copy.  Do any list members know that book or have guesses
about what inspired Lewis's memetic warfare?

(Btw, I recommend the trilogy _Out_of_the_Silent_Planet_, _Perelandra_,
and _That_Hideous_Strength_ for Lewis's entertaining interpretation, _not_
allegory, of fairly ordinary Christianity in science-fictional language.)
