Mike Wiik writes:
> by force. When does it cease to be a "hot" property?. Please clue me.

  It isn't hot. The fact that it was "stolen" centuries ago does not matter.
Those people are dead now and modern society has placed
hunter-gather societies in the trash heap. The lineage has been reset.
How are we going to return property to people who are no longer alive and
people who themselves didn't belive they "owned" the land. I'm non condemning
people who lost their lives due to imperialism, but it was an inevitable
conflict. It's not at all obvious that property is a modern invention.
Atleast some idea of "possession" of land (while occupied) has been
around for millennia atleast. Modern deeds of ownership are just
a more efficient way of dealing with land possession. Memetically
transfering land through deeds instead of resettling on it is much more
efficient.

  The property as theft idea has always been bogus because
it is a self referrential statement. For theft to exist, property
has to exist. If all property is stolen, then someone had to
originally own it, but that person or persons would also be thieves
by definition. If no one originally owned it, then why is it stolen?

  How far to take this analogy. If you breath in air you are 
"stealing". Plants "steal" minerals by mining them with their root
systems. Bacterium steal water molecules from the air. Property
as theft is dumb. And the alternatives are vastly inferior.
Maybe absolute property rights aren't the answer, but it is
quite clear that some sort of private enterprise/property system
is the answer.



  
 
