Date: Wed, 17 Mar 93 15:49:14 PST
From: janzen@mprgate.mpr.ca (Martin Janzen)
Subject: PSYCH: Re. Myers-Briggs type Indicator

I took this test a few weeks ago, after reading about it in Bruce
Tognazzini's "Tog on Interface".  As Eric Raymond and Simon! Levy have
mentioned, the distribution of MB personality types among people who
develop software turns out to be quite different than the distribution
in the general population.  The reason that "Tog" discusses this in a
book on interface design is to point out that those of us who work on
user interfaces should keep in mind that not all (and in fact, very few)
people think and communicate in the same way that we do; nor are they
necessarily motivated by the same things.  Also, it underscores his
arguments in favor of extensive testing, with more than one user (or
kind of user).

In the session I attended, the psychologist explained that MBTI is
considered highly reliable, having been in use for over forty years.
The fact that it is based on Jungian personality theory rather than on
pure statistics was mentioned as a feature, and so was the fact that it
was designed for use with mentally healthy subjects.

Andrew Hall points out that it's easy to falsify the results, and this
certainly is true; especially for someone already familiar with MBTI.
In fact, on the answer sheet I used, the boxes corresponding to each
option for each of the four types were offset (though each question's
weighting was hidden).  So if you wanted to pretend to be an "I" type,
you could just go down the E/I column and check all of the boxes on the
right-hand side.

However, I don't think that this necessarily makes the whole test
worthless.  Remember that it was intended for healthy subjects, not
psychopaths out to fool the tester into releasing them.  The session I
attended, at least, was offered with the intention of increasing
people's understanding of themselves and of others, so most people would
find it a waste of time and money to lie.

Dave Cake writes:
>And finally, a note of synchronicity. [...]

How Jungian!  Sort of fitting... :-)

>[...] So perhaps I could ask him to get some feedback from the
>psychiatrists and clinical psychologists in his group?

I'd be interested in hearing their feedback, so unless you get volumes
of mail denouncing the topic as irrelevant to this list, Dave, please do
ask him! 

And yes, like Simon!, I turn out to be yet another INTJ computer
programmer.  Especially strong I and T scores; I'm sure that at least
these two would be highly repeatable if I were to do the test again.

So what?  Well, it was interesting to find out that the INTJ type is
shared by only about 1% of the general population -- helps to explain
why it's so hard to find others with whom I feel any real rapport.  A
bit discouraging at first, actually.  And the scarcity of NT types
doesn't bode well for the rapid acceptance and spread of reason-based
sets of memes, like Extropianism or Objectivism.

But when dealing with others, I can at least understand that they
probably *don't* think as I do, and can consciously try to compensate
for this instead of writing them off immediately because of their
different personality types, and thus different styles of interaction.
Also, this test result can be seen as indicating those aspects of my
personality which are *not* my strong points, but which I could work to
develop if I so choose.

--
Martin Janzen
janzen@mprgate.mpr.ca

