Date: Wed, 17 Mar 93 18:54:38 CST
From: twb3@midway.uchicago.edu (Tom Morrow)
Subject: ECON: Tragedy of the commons

>In order to avoid the tragedy of the
>commons in protecting an endangered species, there must be some way
>for the people who expend the effort to save the species to obtain
>ownership in the species.  

Why is there a commons?  Are you asserting that the mere existence of a
species is a good enjoyed by a class of people who cannot be excluded from
that benefit?  What good do they so enjoy?  

Here's one plausible answer:  They get the warm fuzzies from just knowing
the species continues to exist.  But the mere fact that a good produces
positive externalities does not mean that a tragedy of the commons in
imminent.  Almost all forms of property emit positive externalities. 
Example:  Abe owns a cool car, and others enjoy admiring its fine lines. 
Example:  Betty owns a restaurant, and passerbys often relish the tasty
odors wafting from its doors.  Etc.

The question is whether or not the owners of such goods can internalize
enough of the benefits to ensure the continued existence of these goods. 
If so, no tragedy looms.

This is surely the case with endangered species.  In fact, it is
*especially* the case the more that the species becomes endangered.  The
rarer the animal, the more valuable it becomes for breeding ("Zoos!  Call
now to ensure that your next generation of specimens has the most robust
set of genes available!"), for ecoentertainment ("Come see the last living
flophog!"), and for genetic mining ("I'm sure the Sandoz company will be
interested in learning more about this promising source of new
pharmaceutical breakthroughs."). 

>Unfortunately, ownership of individuals
>doesn't really suffice, because the value of the species is (according
>to environmentalist theory) in the *existance* of the species, not in
>any particular individual of the species.
>
>Dale

Again, what sort of value are you speaking about?  Why should I care about
the species apart from its individual members?  Please explicate in a
manner that does not ascribe moral value to species qua species.

